WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:06.287
FedRAMP baselines define security control expectations for cloud service offerings based on impact and use case.

00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:10.540
They connect federal cloud risk to implementation, evidence, assessment, authorization support,

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:11.700
and continuous monitoring.

00:00:11.700 --> 00:00:17.113
A baseline is not a marketing label and not a guarantee of perfect security.

00:00:17.113 --> 00:00:21.753
It is a structured starting point for demonstrating that a cloud service

00:00:21.753 --> 00:00:24.847
is protected and operated appropriately for federal use.

00:00:24.847 --> 00:00:26.780
Impact thinking is the foundation.

00:00:26.780 --> 00:00:27.553
Confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:27.553 --> 00:00:33.353
and availability help teams understand what kind of harm could result from a security incident.

00:00:33.353 --> 00:00:34.900
Low, tailored low-impact, Moderate,

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:39.540
and High concepts help align security expectations with data sensitivity, mission impact,

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.473
service complexity, and agency use.

00:00:41.473 --> 00:00:45.727
The right baseline should reflect system risk, not convenience or prestige.

00:00:45.727 --> 00:00:51.527
Baselines are grounded in NIST control concepts but applied through the realities of cloud architecture.

00:00:51.527 --> 00:00:53.847
Shared responsibility, inherited controls, customer responsibilities,

00:00:53.847 --> 00:00:56.940
control implementation statements, and evidence boundaries all matter.

00:00:56.940 --> 00:01:01.580
Two services under the same baseline can look different because their architectures,

00:01:01.580 --> 00:01:04.673
service models, dependencies, and operating processes are different.

00:01:04.673 --> 00:01:07.380
The baseline tells teams what to address;

00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.473
the system design determines how it is addressed.

00:01:10.473 --> 00:01:14.340
The practical takeaway is simple: baseline work is operational work.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.820
It requires honest scope, real control implementation, assessable documentation,

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.687
evidence from actual workflows, vulnerability management, incident readiness, change awareness,

00:01:21.687 --> 00:01:22.847
and continuous monitoring.

00:01:22.847 --> 00:01:25.553
The goal is not to collect paperwork.

00:01:25.553 --> 00:01:31.740
The goal is to help agencies make informed risk decisions about cloud services that are documented,

00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:35.220
tested, monitored, and operated for their federal mission context.
